 ('Show ∫ₐᵃf(x)dx = 0 using the fundamental theorem.',['Assume f has an antiderivative F on the interval.','The fundamental theorem gives ∫ₐᵃf(x)dx = F(a)−F(a).','Both endpoint values are the same number.','Their difference is zero.','No integration constant remains because it cancels in the same subtraction.'],'0.',169),
 ('Show ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx = ∫ₐᵇf(y)dy using the definite-integral definition.',['Partition the same interval [a,b] into n equal pieces.','Both variables use the same width Δ = (b−a)/n.','Choose the same numerical sample values rᵢ in each piece.','The x sum and the y sum are both Σf(rᵢ)Δ; changing a bound variable’s name changes no summand.','For an integrable f, the equal sums have equal limits, proving the identity.'],'The two definite integrals are equal.',169),
 ('Show ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx = −∫ᵇₐf(x)dx using the fundamental theorem.',['Let F′ = f.','Evaluate the forward integral: F(b)−F(a).','Evaluate the reversed integral: F(a)−F(b).','Negating the reversed result gives F(b)−F(a).','Therefore the forward integral equals the negative of the reversed integral, including when a = b.'],'Forward and reversed integrals have opposite signs.',169),
 ('Show ∫ₐᵇf = ∫ₐᶜf+∫ᶜᵇf using the definite-integral definition.',['Assume a < c < b and f is continuous, hence integrable, on [a,b].','Choose partitions of [a,c] and [c,b] whose largest widths both tend to zero.','Combine the two partitions into one partition of [a,b].','The combined Riemann sum is exactly the sum of the two smaller sums, because every subinterval belongs to one side.','Take the limits: the combined sum tends to ∫ₐᵇf, while the separate sums tend to ∫ₐᶜf and ∫ᶜᵇf.','The limit of their sum is the sum of their limits, proving additivity. Equal spacing is not needed for this proof.'],'The integral over the whole interval is the sum of the two subinterval integrals.',170)
